{"input_text": "Question: Suppose we have three independent exponential random variables $A$, $B$ and $C$  with respective parameters $1$, $2$ and $3$.  
 Calculate $P(A<B<C)$. 
 The hint says this problem could be solved with calculus and without calculus. I am really curious how to approach it with different methods. 

Answer: I have written a program to test the probabilities for three independent exponential random variables  A ,  B  and  C  with respective parameters λ a  = 1, λ b  = 2, λ c  = 3. Here are the results: 
 P (A < B < C) = 0.3275
P (A < C < B) = 0.2181
P (B < A < C) = 0.2047
P (B < C < A) = 0.0681
P (C < A < B) = 0.1211
P (C < B < A) = 0.0603

 P (A < B < C)  is not 1/15. 
 Here is the source code of the program: 
 Random Rand = new Random(2015);

public Double GetExponential(Double mean)
{
    return -mean * Math.Log(Rand.NextDouble());
}

var totalCase = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; ++i)
{
    var p1 = GetExponential(1);
    var p2 = GetExponential(2);
    var p3 = GetExponential(3);

    if (p1 < p2 && p2 < p3)
        ++totalCase;
}
Console.WriteLine(totalCase / 1000000.0);

