{"input_text": "Question: I would like to construct a measure to calculate the sparseness of a vector of length $k$.  
 Let $X = [x_i]$ be a vector of length $k$ such that there exist an $x_i \neq 0$ . Assume $x_i \geq 0$ for all $i$. 
 One such measure I came across is defined as $$\frac{\sqrt{k} - \frac{\|X\|_1}{{\|X\|_2}}} {\sqrt{k} -1}\;,$$ where $\|X\|_1$ is $L_1$ norm and $\|X\|_2$ is $L_2$ norm. 
 Here, $\operatorname{Sparseness}(X) = 0$ whenever the vector is dense (all components are equal and non-zero) and $\operatorname{Sparseness}(X) = 1$ whenever the vector is sparse (only one component is non zero). 
 This  post only explains the when $0$ and $1$ achieved by the above mentioned measure. 
 Is there any other function defining the sparseness of the vector. 

Answer: $
\def\o{{\tt1}}
\def\eo{\|a\|_\o}\def\et{\|a\|_2}
\def\xo{\|x\|_\o}\def\xt{\|x\|_2}
\def\Eo{\|A\|_\o}\def\Et{\|A\|_2}
\def\Xo{\|X\|_\o}\def\Xt{\|X\|_2}
\def\H{{\cal H}}\def\K{{\cal K}}
\def\L{\left}\def\R{\right}
\def\LR#1{\L(#1\R)}
\def\BR#1{\Big(#1\Big)}
\def\bR#1{\big(#1\big)}
\def\fracLR#1#2{\LR{\frac{#1}{#2}}}
$ Let  $a\in{\mathbb R}^{n}$  denote the all-ones vector,
then
 $$\eqalign{
\eo = n\qquad{\rm and}\qquad \et = {\sqrt n} }$$ 
This means that the Hoyer sparsity can be written as
 $$\eqalign{
\H(x) &= \frac{\et\cdot\xt\;-\;\xo}{\et\cdot\xt\;-\;\xt} \\
}$$ 
However, an equally good sparsity measure is
 $$\eqalign{
\K(x) &= \frac{\eo\cdot\xt^2\;-\;\xo^2}{\eo\cdot\xt^2\;-\;\xt^2} \\\\
}$$ 
Although  $\K(x)\ne\H(x)$  in general,
the extrema for both functions are the same
 $$\eqalign{
\K(a) &= \H(a) &= 0 \\
\K(e_k) &= \H(e_k) &= \o \\
}$$ 
where  $e_k$  is any cartesian basis vector. 

 Note that both functions extend naturally to the matrix case
 $X\in{\mathbb R}^{n\times m}$ 
 $$\eqalign{
\H(X) &= \frac{\Et\cdot\Xt\;-\;\Xo}{\Et\cdot\Xt\;-\;\Xt} \\
\\
\K(X) &= \frac{\Eo\cdot\Xt^2\;-\;\Xo^2}{\Eo\cdot\Xt^2\;-\;\Xt^2} \\
}$$ 
where  $\Xo$  is the Manhattan norm,  $\Xt$  is the Frobenius norm,
and  $A$  is all-ones. 
 The extrema are
 $$\eqalign{
\K(A) &= \H(A) &= 0 \\
\K(E_{ij}) &= \H(E_{ij}) &= \o \\
E_{ij} &= e_i\,e_j^T \\
}$$ 
 Update 
 Choose  $\alpha,\beta\in\{{\tt1},2,\infty\},\;\alpha\!\ne\!\beta,\,$  and  $\,p\in{\mathbb R}\;$  and define the function
 $$\eqalign{
F(X) &= \frac{\big\|X\big\|^p_\alpha}{\big\|X\big\|^p_\beta} \\
}$$ 
Use this function to construct a generalized sparsity estimate
 $$\eqalign{
{\cal S}(X) \;=\; \frac{F(A) \;-\; F(X)}{F(A) \;-\; F(E_{ij})},
\qquad {\cal S}(A)=0,
\;\; {\cal S}(E_{ij})=\o
}$$ 
Notice that for any choice of parameters
 $\;F(E_{ij}) = \o$ 
